{"page":345,"blocks":[{"x":0.61675,"y":0.05257,"w":0.33476,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"5.4 The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus 331"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.09306,"w":0.59305,"h":0.0446,"fs":0.01311,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"Proof of Theorem 4 We prove the Fundamental Theorem, Part 1, by applying the definition of the derivative directly to the function F(x), when x and x + h are in (a, b). This means writing out the difference quotient"},{"x":0.60069,"y":0.14044,"w":0.11299,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"F(x + h) - F(x)"},{"x":0.6531,"y":0.15045,"w":0.29677,"h":0.02006,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"h (3)"},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.17183,"w":0.58895,"h":0.01443,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"and showing that its limit as h S 0 is the number ƒ(x) for each x in (a, b). Doing so, we find"},{"x":0.58673,"y":0.19416,"w":0.11299,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"F(x + h) - F(x)"},{"x":0.50245,"y":0.20348,"w":0.08098,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"F′(x) = lim"},{"x":0.56031,"y":0.2139,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.62566,"y":0.23021,"w":0.02061,"h":0.00942,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x+h"},{"x":0.58709,"y":0.23337,"w":0.01847,"h":0.0305,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"1 h c"},{"x":0.73375,"y":0.23337,"w":0.04726,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(t) dtd"},{"x":0.54335,"y":0.24312,"w":0.04011,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= lim"},{"x":0.6463,"y":0.24313,"w":0.05732,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(t) dt -"},{"x":0.56035,"y":0.25355,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.61707,"y":0.27241,"w":0.02061,"h":0.00942,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x+h"},{"x":0.58709,"y":0.27898,"w":0.0283,"h":0.03065,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"1 h L"},{"x":0.54335,"y":0.28533,"w":0.04011,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= lim"},{"x":0.63771,"y":0.286,"w":0.26586,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(t) dt. Table 5.6, Rule 5"},{"x":0.56035,"y":0.29576,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.31543,"w":0.59307,"h":0.04348,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"According to the Mean Value Theorem for Definite Integrals, the value before taking the limit in the last expression is one of the values taken on by ƒ in the interval between x and x + h. That is, for some number c in this interval,"},{"x":0.61432,"y":0.36322,"w":0.02061,"h":0.00942,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x+h"},{"x":0.58433,"y":0.36978,"w":0.0283,"h":0.03065,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"1 h L"},{"x":0.63496,"y":0.37613,"w":0.31491,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(t) dt = ƒ(c). (4)"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.40459,"w":0.59303,"h":0.02978,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"As h S 0, x + h approaches x, forcing c to approach x also (because c is trapped between x and x + h). Since ƒ is continuous at x, ƒ(c) approaches ƒ(x):"},{"x":0.58425,"y":0.44008,"w":0.36563,"h":0.02178,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"lim hS0 ƒ(c) = ƒ(x). (5)"},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.46762,"w":0.15026,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"In conclusion, we have"},{"x":0.68271,"y":0.48471,"w":0.02061,"h":0.00942,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x+h"},{"x":0.65472,"y":0.49128,"w":0.02631,"h":0.03065,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"1 hL"},{"x":0.57012,"y":0.49763,"w":0.08098,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"F′(x) = lim"},{"x":0.70335,"y":0.49829,"w":0.04036,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(t) dt"},{"x":0.62798,"y":0.50805,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.61206,"y":0.52832,"w":0.04011,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= lim"},{"x":0.62905,"y":0.52899,"w":0.18376,"h":0.02112,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"hS0 ƒ(c) Eq. (4)"},{"x":0.61206,"y":0.5539,"w":0.20075,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"= ƒ(x). Eq. (5)"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.57572,"w":0.58775,"h":0.01467,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"If x = a or b, then the limit of Equation (3) is interpreted as a one-sided limit with h S 0+"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.59107,"w":0.59293,"h":0.01467,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"or h S 0-, respectively. Then Theorem 1 in Section 3.2 shows that F is continuous over"},{"x":0.36504,"y":0.59786,"w":0.21759,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"3a, b4 . This concludes the proof."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.63352,"w":0.43327,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Fundamental Theorem, Part 2 (The Evaluation Theorem)"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.65817,"w":0.5931,"h":0.04348,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"We now come to the second part of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. This part describes how to evaluate definite integrals without having to calculate limits of Riemann sums. Instead we find and evaluate an antiderivative at the upper and lower limits of integration."},{"x":0.39285,"y":0.73802,"w":0.52841,"h":0.02899,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"THEOREM 4 (Continued)—The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2 If ƒ is continuous over 3a, b4 and F is any antiderivative of ƒ on 3a, b4 , then"},{"x":0.59048,"y":0.78807,"w":0.15657,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(x) dx = F(b) - F(a)."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.85375,"w":0.58894,"h":0.01391,"fs":0.01311,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Proof Part 1 of the Fundamental Theorem tells us that an antiderivative of ƒ exists, namely"},{"x":0.59632,"y":0.88488,"w":0.04695,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"G(x) ="},{"x":0.67339,"y":0.88555,"w":0.0444,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(t) dt."},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.91941,"w":0.59305,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Thus, if F is any antiderivative of ƒ, then F(x) = G(x) + C for some constant C for a 6 x 6 b (by Corollary 2 of the Mean Value Theorem for Derivatives, Section 4.2)."}]}